WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:11.200
When most people think of ethics or morals, they&nbsp;
think of rules for distinguishing between right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:15.920
and wrong, such as the Golden Rule, "Do unto&nbsp;
others as you would have them do unto you";&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:20.160
a code of professional conduct&nbsp;
such as the Hippocratic Oath,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:27.360
"First of all, do no harm"; a religious creed like&nbsp;
the Ten Commandments, "Thou Shalt not kill...";&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:34.960
or, a wise aphorisms like the sayings of&nbsp;
Confucius. This is the most common way of defining&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:40.000
"ethics", norms for conduct that distinguish&nbsp;
between acceptable and unacceptable behavior.

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:45.760
Most people learn ethical norms at home, at&nbsp;
school, in church, or in other social settings.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:51.600
Although most people acquire their sense of right&nbsp;
and wrong during childhood, moral development&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:56.160
occurs throughout life as human beings pass&nbsp;
through different stages of growth as they mature.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.120 --> 00:01:02.000
Ethical norms are so ubiquitous that one might&nbsp;
be tempted to regard them as simple commonsense.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:06.640
On the other hand, if morality&nbsp;
were nothing more than commonsense,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:10.160
then why are there so many ethical&nbsp;
disputes and issues in our society?

00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:14.720
One plausible explanation of these&nbsp;
disagreements is that all people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:18.240
recognize some common ethical&nbsp;
norms but interpret, apply,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:22.080
and balance them in different ways in light&nbsp;
of their own values and life experiences.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:28.480
For example, two people could agree that murder is&nbsp;
wrong but disagree about the morality of abortion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:31.840
because they have different understandings&nbsp;
of what it means to be a human being.

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:39.120
Most societies also have legal rules that&nbsp;
govern behavior, but ethical norms tend to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:45.520
be broader and more informal than laws. Although&nbsp;
most societies use laws to enforce widely accepted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:51.840
moral standards, ethics and law are not the&nbsp;
same. An action may be legal but unethical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:58.240
or illegal but ethical. We can also use&nbsp;
ethical concepts and principles to criticize,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:07.520
evaluate, propose, or interpret laws. Indeed,&nbsp;
in the last century, many social reformers have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:13.520
urged citizens to disobey laws they regarded&nbsp;
as immoral or unjust laws. Peaceful civil&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:18.560
disobedience is an ethical way of protesting&nbsp;
laws or expressing political viewpoints.

00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:26.240
Many different disciplines, institutions, and&nbsp;
professions have standards for behavior that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:31.760
suit their particular aims and goals. These&nbsp;
standards also help members of the discipline&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:36.640
to coordinate their actions or activities and to&nbsp;
establish the public's trust of the discipline.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:42.000
For instance, ethical standards govern&nbsp;
conduct in medicine, law, engineering,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:47.440
and business. Ethical norms also serve&nbsp;
the aims or goals of research and apply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:52.080
to people who conduct scientific research&nbsp;
or other scholarly or creative activities.

00:02:55.760 --> 00:03:04.240
There are several reasons why it is important&nbsp;
to adhere to ethical norms in research. First,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.960 --> 00:03:10.800
norms promote the aims of research, such as&nbsp;
knowledge, truth, and avoidance of error.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:15.200
For example, prohibitions against fabricating,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:19.840
falsifying, or misrepresenting research&nbsp;
data promote the truth and minimize error.

00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:29.440
Second, since research often involves a great&nbsp;
deal of cooperation and coordination among many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:33.760
different people in different disciplines and&nbsp;
institutions, ethical standards promote the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:38.560
values that are essential to collaborative&nbsp;
work, such as trust, accountability,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.200 --> 00:03:45.520
mutual respect, and fairness. For&nbsp;
example, many ethical norms in research,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:51.120
such as guidelines for authorship, copyright&nbsp;
and patenting policies, data sharing policies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:55.920
and confidentiality rules in peer review,&nbsp;
are designed to protect intellectual property&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.920 --> 00:04:01.120
interests while encouraging collaboration.&nbsp;
Most researchers want to receive credit for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:05.920
their contributions and do not want to have&nbsp;
their ideas stolen or disclosed prematurely.

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:14.960
Third, many of the ethical norms help to ensure&nbsp;
that researchers can be held accountable to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.960 --> 00:04:20.960
public. For instance, federal policies on&nbsp;
research misconduct, conflicts of interest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.960 --> 00:04:25.760
the human subjects protections, and animal&nbsp;
care and use are necessary in order to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:30.160
sure that researchers who are funded by public&nbsp;
money can be held accountable to the public.

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:40.400
Fourth, ethical norms in research also help to&nbsp;
build public support for research. People are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:45.360
more likely to fund a research project if they&nbsp;
can trust the quality and integrity of research.

00:04:49.040 --> 00:04:53.600
Finally, many of the norms of research&nbsp;
promote a variety of other important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.600 --> 00:04:59.280
moral and social values, such as social&nbsp;
responsibility, human rights, animal welfare,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.280 --> 00:05:04.080
compliance with the law, and public&nbsp;
health and safety. Ethical lapses in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.080 --> 00:05:10.480
research can significantly harm human and animal&nbsp;
subjects, students, and the public. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.480 --> 00:05:15.280
a researcher who fabricates data in a&nbsp;
clinical trial may harm or even kill patients.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.240 --> 00:05:22.080
A researcher who fails to abide by regulations&nbsp;
and guidelines relating to radiation or biological&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:26.560
safety may jeopardize his health and safety or&nbsp;
the health and safety of staff and students.

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:33.280
Given the importance of ethics&nbsp;
for the conduct of research,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.280 --> 00:05:38.400
it should come as no surprise that many different&nbsp;
professional associations, government agencies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.400 --> 00:05:43.680
and universities have adopted specific codes,&nbsp;
rules, and policies relating to research ethics.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.640 --> 00:05:51.840
These include the Nuremberg Code and the World&nbsp;
Medical Association's Declaration of Helsinki.

00:05:53.360 --> 00:05:58.800
In summary, although codes, policies, and&nbsp;
principals are very important and useful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:02.320
like any set of rules, they&nbsp;
do not cover every situation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.320 --> 00:06:05.840
they often conflict, and they&nbsp;
require considerable interpretation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.800 --> 00:06:11.600
It is therefore important for researchers&nbsp;
to learn how to interpret, assess, and apply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.600 --> 00:06:19.840
various research rules and how to make decisions&nbsp;
and to act ethically in various situations.

